  "id": "DrugBank_DB03754 - MONDO_5642",
  "verbalization": "Tromethamine appears to be a potential candidate for repurposing in the treatment of atopic conjunctivitis, as it is indicated for papillary conjunctivitis, which in turn is linked to atopic conjunctivitis through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Tromethamine may exert a therapeutic effect by targeting the underlying inflammatory processes associated with papillary conjunctivitis, which is a common precursor to atopic conjunctivitis. The presence of multiple indications through which Tromethamine connects to atopic conjunctivitis—via alcaftadine, emedastine, and ketotifen—indicates a possible shared mechanism of action, possibly involving anti-inflammatory or immunomodulatory effects. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a plausible, though not yet fully elucidated, mechanism of action that could be relevant to the treatment of atopic conjunctivitis. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Tromethamine may have a role in modulating inflammatory responses in the conjunctiva, though further mechanistic studies are needed to confirm this.",
